{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And I will establish My covenant</b> Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 6:18): it is a sign that HaShem swore to him that he and his sons would not die in the Flood, and if this had not been written beforehand explicitly, we would have found [something similar] in the repetition of the Torah (Deuteronomy 1:22), \"Let us send men before us.\" And the word \"וַהֲקִימֹתִי\" (and I will establish) means: I will fulfill My oath. And it seems closer to me that this covenant is an allusion to the bow [the rainbow]. And the meaning of בְּרִית (covenant) is agreement and a matter chosen by two parties, derived from \"בְּרוּ לָכֶם\" (1 Samuel 17:8, \"choose for yourselves\"), and the word is related [as cognate] to בָּרָה (to choose) and to בְּרִירָה (selection). And likewise \"שְׁבִית יַעֲקֹב\" (Psalms 85:2, \"the captivity of Jacob\"), \"וּבְנוֹתָיו בַּשְּׁבִית\" (Numbers 21:29, \"and his daughters in captivity\") — all these are his [Ibn Ezra's] words. And some say that בְּרִית means a cut boundary; all these are his words. But it is more correct according to the plain sense that the meaning of \"וַהֲקִימֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי\" is to say: at the time when the Flood comes, there will be an enduring covenant with you, that you shall come into the ark — you and your household and two of all flesh — to keep alive, that is, that you shall live there and endure to go out from there to life, and the covenant is the word of HaShem when He decrees with an utterance, without condition or reservation, and He will fulfill it. And He mentioned the covenant and He mentioned that it would be enduring, and this is like the expression \"קִיְּמוּ וְקִבְּלוּ הַיְּהוּדִים עֲלֵיהֶם וְעַל זַרְעָם\" (Esther 9:27, \"the Jews established and accepted upon themselves and upon their seed\"), for they accepted upon themselves a matter to make it enduring. And by way of truth, the covenant is from the world [above], and the word is derived from \"בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים\" (Genesis 1:1), and behold בְּרִיתִי (My covenant) is like בְּרִיָּתִי (My creation), and the word is as though [it were] בְּרִיאָתִי, for it is adjacent to the worlds that existed before us; He commands that it stand and be with the righteous. And likewise \"וַאֲנִי הִנְנִי מֵקִים אֶת בְּרִיתִי אִתְּכֶם\" (Genesis 9:9), \"בְּרִיתִי הָיְתָה אִתּוֹ\" (Malachi 2:5). And the enlightened will understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "Ramban attributes to Ibn Ezra three distinct proposed etymologies for בְּרִית: (1) agreement/chosen thing from ברה, (2) cognate with שביה (captivity) as parallel construct, (3) a cut boundary. The phrase 'סמוך ומוכרת' is rendered here as 'related (as cognate) to ברה and to ברירה,' following the standard identification of these as Ibn Ezra's lexicographical parallels."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:20:54.859709725Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}